.TH CYAML_CLONE 3 "2026-09-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_clone \- produce a deep independent copy of a DOM subtree
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

cyaml cyaml_clone(cyaml node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Copy node and all of its descendants.  The clone uses the same allocator
as the source tree and carries over unchanged the tag of each node that
has one (see cyaml_node_tag(3)), and every dictionary of the clone
keeps the member order of its source (see cyaml_dictionary_first(3)).
A change to the clone does not affect the original, and a change to the
original does not affect the clone.

node need not come from cyaml_parse*().  A tree that the caller builds
directly with cyaml_create_list_mp(3) and cyaml_dictionary_set(3) obeys
the same 500-level nesting limit that cyaml_parse*(3) applies at parse
time, and cyaml_serialize(3) and cyaml_serialize_flow(3) apply that same
limit on their own.  This function refuses a tree that is nested deeper
than the limit and returns NULL for it.  Because it walks the source
with an explicit worklist instead of by recursion, the call stack that
it needs is a constant that does not grow with the nesting depth: the
limit bounds the work of one call, not the stack that the caller must
give.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
node  Root of the subtree to copy.  The function accepts NULL, in which
      case it returns NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Root of the new tree, which the caller owns, or NULL when the function
runs out of memory or when node is nested more than 500 levels deep.
The caller cannot tell the two causes apart, because this function has
no separate error-string channel.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_destroy (3),
.BR cyaml_dictionary_first (3),
.BR cyaml_node_tag (3)
